.TH WSL-DNS-FORWARD 8 "November 7, 2026" "wsl-dns-forward 1.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
wsl-dns-forward \- make .local (mDNS) host names resolve inside WSL2
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsl-dns-forward
.RB [ \-D ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR ADDR ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR HOST[:PORT] ]...
.RB [ \-U
.IR USER ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR POWERSHELL ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B wsl-dns-forward
.B \-h
|
.B \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
mDNS (multicast DNS, also sold as Bonjour or Rendezvous) gives every machine on a local network a
.I name.local
host name without needing a DNS server.
.PP
WSL2 runs behind a private virtual network that cannot see the multicast traffic mDNS depends on, so
.I name.local
lookups from inside WSL2 fail or answer unreliably, and the Windows 10 versions of WSL do not have the
.B dnsTunneling
feature that newer Windows releases use to hand such lookups to Windows.
.B wsl-dns-forward
fixes this with a small DNS server on the loopback address.
Questions about
.I .local
names are answered by asking a helper program running in Windows (PowerShell, started through WSL's interop),
which sends the mDNS query out of the real network adapter.
Every other question is passed unchanged to the normal upstream DNS server.
Programs in WSL2 need no changes: once
.I /etc/resolv.conf
points at the daemon, ssh, ping, CUPS and the rest resolve
.I .local
names like any other.
.PP
The daemon listens on UDP and TCP, serves each connection for one query, and never blocks one client on another.
It starts as root only to open port 53, then switches to an unprivileged user.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-D
Run in the background.
Messages go to the log file (see
.BR \-L )
instead of the terminal.
A small supervisor process stays behind as root and restarts the worker if it dies, waiting one second
after the first failure and doubling up to thirty seconds while it keeps dying young.
The pid file holds the supervisor's pid, and signalling it stops both.
.TP
.BI \-l " ADDR"
IPv4 address to listen on.
Default
.IR 127.0.0.1 .
Keep it on the loopback address unless you know why not.
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT"
Port to listen on, 1 to 65535.
Default 53.
.TP
.BI \-u " HOST[:PORT]"
Upstream DNS server for names that are not
.IR .local .
May be given up to six times.
With no
.BR \-u ,
the daemon builds the list itself: the non-loopback
.B nameserver
lines of
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
then the WSL default gateway (where Windows 10 runs WSL's DNS proxy), then 10.255.255.254 (the DNS tunnel
address of Windows 11 22H2 and later), skipping duplicates.
Whichever server answered last is tried first.
.TP
.BI \-U " USER"
User to run as after the port is open.
Default
.IR nobody .
.B \-U root
keeps root (not recommended).
Ignored when not started as root.
.TP
.BI \-w " POWERSHELL"
Path to
.I powershell.exe
as seen from WSL.
Default: the path chosen at build time,
normally
.IR /mnt/c/Windows/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe .
If that is not executable, the daemon tries
.I powershell.exe
from
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE"
File that remembers the last good address of each name.
Default
.IR /var/cache/wsl-dns-forward/last-good .
.TP
.BI \-P " FILE"
Write the process id to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-L " FILE"
Log file used with
.BR \-D .
Default
.IR /var/log/wsl-dns-forward.log .
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.SH OPERATION
.SS How a query is handled
.IP \(bu 3
A query for
.IR name.local ,
type A (an IPv4 address) is answered from the cache while the entry is under two minutes old.
Entries in the last quarter of that time are refreshed in the background, so names in regular use never wait.
An expired or unknown name is looked up before it is answered, waiting up to two seconds when an older address
is known and up to six otherwise, so a device whose address changed is not answered with the old one.
.IP \(bu 3
AAAA (IPv6) queries for
.I .local
names are answered with the device's global IPv6 addresses (up to four) when the system can route IPv6, that
is, when it has a global-scope IPv6 address and an IPv6 default route, as WSL2 has in mirrored networking mode.
Link-local, multicast, loopback and IPv4-mapped addresses are never returned.
Otherwise, as in WSL2 NAT mode, which has no IPv6 route to the LAN, the answer is empty and successful, because
an IPv6 address would only give "Network is unreachable"; the empty answer makes programs use IPv4 at once.
The check is repeated every few seconds, and the daemon logs which mode it is in.
A host with only IPv4 addresses gets an empty AAAA answer, and a host with only IPv6 addresses gets an empty A answer.
Other record types get an empty answer as well.
.IP \(bu 3
.I .local
names may contain only letters, digits, hyphens and underscores.
Anything else is refused.
.IP \(bu 3
All other queries are relayed unchanged to the upstream server, and the reply is checked
(same source, same ID, same question) before it is passed back.
.IP \(bu 3
A reply is built for the TCP client the same way, but over TCP only one query is served per connection.
Answers longer than the upstream gives over UDP are not retried over TCP.
.SS Fallbacks
Each of these is automatic.
When one is used, a diagnostic is logged (see DIAGNOSTICS) so you can find out why.
.IP 1. 3
.B powershell.exe
is looked up at the configured path first, then in
.BR PATH .
.IP 2. 3
mDNS queries are sent from every connected network adapter that has an IPv4 address, adapters with a default
gateway first, skipping WSL's own virtual switches.
Up to three rounds are made before giving up.
.IP 3. 3
If mDNS gets no reply, the Windows resolver (Resolve-DnsName) is tried, which also covers unicast
.I .local
entries.
.IP 4. 3
If the lookup then fails or times out but an earlier address is known (in memory or in the cache file),
that last-known-good address is returned instead.
.IP 5. 3
If the helper dies, it is restarted on the next lookup, waiting 0.5 s after the first failure and doubling
up to 30 s.
Until it is back, uncached
.I .local
names get SERVFAIL; cached names and ordinary names keep working.
With
.BR \-D ,
a worker that dies is restarted by the supervisor (see
.BR \-D ).
.IP 6. 3
Upstream servers are tried two seconds each, starting with the one that answered last, so a dead entry in
the list costs one delay and is then avoided.
This is what keeps the setup working across a Windows 10 to Windows 11 upgrade, when the saved nameserver
addresses can go stale.
.IP 7. 3
If the daemon itself is not running, programs fall through to the next
.B nameserver
line in
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
so keep your existing nameservers listed after
.IR 127.0.0.1 .
Ordinary names then still resolve, just after a delay.
.SH UPGRADING TO WINDOWS 11
This section describes what happens to a working setup when the computer is upgraded from Windows 10 to
Windows 11.
.SS Short answer
Nothing needs to change, and
.I .local
names keep resolving.
Windows 11 22H2 and later can also do this job itself (DNS tunneling, which is on by default, and optional
mirrored networking with multicast support), so the program may become unnecessary, but it does not stop working.
.SS What carries over
The configuration lives in the Linux distribution:
.I /etc/wsl.conf
and
.I /etc/resolv.conf
are not touched by a Windows upgrade or by
.IR "wsl \-\-update" .
The
.B [boot]
command is supported on Windows 11, so the daemon starts as before.
The Windows half is Windows PowerShell 5.1 and standard .NET sockets, which Windows 11 provides.
It uses the connected network adapters, skipping only WSL's own virtual switches, which are named
.I "vEthernet (WSL...)"
and
.I "vEthernet (Default Switch)"
on both systems.
.SS What changes
With DNS tunneling on, Windows 11 answers WSL's DNS at the virtual address 10.255.255.254 and no longer runs a
DNS proxy at the gateway address that Windows 10 used.
Nameserver lines saved in
.I /etc/resolv.conf
can therefore go stale.
Without
.BR \-u ,
the daemon tries the nameservers in that file, then the gateway, then 10.255.255.254, and remembers the one that
answered, so no edit is required.
The first lookup after the upgrade may wait two seconds on a dead entry and report
.BR W07 ;
later lookups skip it.
.SS What to check
After the upgrade, run
.I "wsl \-\-update"
and
.I "wsl \-\-shutdown"
in Windows, open WSL again, and run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pgrep \-a wsl\-mdns\-forward
getent ahosts printer.local
dig +short example.com
grep upstream /var/log/wsl\-mdns\-forward.log | tail
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.I upstream
lines list the servers the daemon found; with Windows 11's default settings 10.255.255.254 is one of them.
.SS Optional tidying
To remove the one-time delay, delete the dead
.B nameserver
lines from
.I /etc/resolv.conf
(keep
.I "nameserver 127.0.0.1"
first), or pass the right server with
.BR \-u .
.SS Settings that matter
.TP
.B dnsTunneling=false
in
.I %USERPROFILE%\e.wslconfig
turns Windows 11's DNS tunnel off.
The gateway answers DNS again, and it is one of the servers tried.
.TP
.B networkingMode=mirrored
makes WSL share Windows' loopback address, so port 53 must be free on the Windows side.
Otherwise
.B E01
is reported.
It also gives WSL IPv6 routing, so the daemon then answers
.I .local
AAAA queries with IPv6 addresses.
.SS Stopping use of the program
To let Windows 11 manage DNS itself, remove the
.B [boot]
command and
.B generateResolvConf
lines from
.IR /etc/wsl.conf ,
remove
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
and run
.I "wsl \-\-shutdown"
so that WSL recreates it.
Add
.B networkingMode=mirrored
to
.B [wsl2]
in
.I .wslconfig
if multicast should reach Linux directly.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/wsl-dns-forward
The daemon.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wsl-dns-forward/mdns-helper.ps1
The Windows helper script, sent to PowerShell when needed.
.TP
.I /var/cache/wsl-dns-forward/last-good
Last-known-good addresses, one
.I name address
per line.
.TP
.I /var/log/wsl-dns-forward.log
Log (with
.BR \-D ).
.TP
.I /etc/resolv.conf
Must list
.I nameserver 127.0.0.1
first.
.TP
.I /etc/wsl.conf
Where to start the daemon at boot, and where DNS generation is turned off.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WMF_IPV6
.B 0
or
.B 1
forces IPv6 answers off or on, overriding the detection described under OPERATION.
.TP
.B WMF_HELPER_SCRIPT
Use this helper script instead of the installed one.
.TP
.B WMF_HELPER
A shell command to run instead of PowerShell.
For testing.
.TP
.B WMF_FRESH_MS
Milliseconds an address stays fresh (default 120000).
For testing.
.TP
.B WMF_RESOLV_CONF
Read the upstream nameservers from this file instead of
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal stop (SIGTERM or SIGINT) or successful
.B \-h
or
.BR \-V .
.TP
.B 1
Start-up failed: could not listen, switch user, or fork.
.TP
.B 2
Bad command line.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop the daemon cleanly and end the helper.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages look like this and go to standard error (to the log file under
.BR \-D ):
.PP
.nf
2026-11-07 13:04:52 [E04] cannot run powershell.exe: No such file or directory
    why: WSL could not start Windows PowerShell ...
    try: test it with: powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command 1 ...
    see: man 8 wsl-dns-forward, section DIAGNOSTICS, entry E04
.fi
.PP
Repeats of the same problem are shown at most once every ten seconds.
.TP
.B E01
Cannot open the listening socket.
Port 53 needs root; if the port is taken, find who has it with
.IR "ss -lunp 'sport = :53'" .
Use
.B \-p
to try another port.
.TP
.B E02
The helper script is missing, unreadable, not plain ASCII, or longer than 11000 characters once comments and
indentation are stripped (it travels on the Windows command line, which is limited to 32767 characters).
Reinstall, or set
.BR WMF_HELPER_SCRIPT .
.TP
.B E03
Out of processes, memory or descriptors while starting the helper.
.TP
.B E04
WSL could not run PowerShell.
Run
.I "powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command 1"
by hand.
If it fails, interop is probably disabled: look for
.I enabled=false
under
.I [interop]
in
.IR /etc/wsl.conf ,
then restart WSL with
.I "wsl --shutdown"
from Windows.
If PowerShell is in an unusual place, pass
.BR \-w .
.TP
.B E06
No upstream DNS server is known.
Pass one with
.BR \-u ,
or list your nameservers after
.I nameserver 127.0.0.1
in
.I /etc/resolv.conf
(the saved copy is
.IR /etc/resolv.conf.pre-wsl-dns-forward ).
.TP
.B E09
The run user does not exist or could not be switched to.
Check with
.IR "id nobody" .
.TP
.B E13
A bad option value (port, upstream address, or an upstream that is the daemon itself).
.TP
.B W03
.I powershell.exe
was not at the configured path and
.B PATH
was used instead.
Pass
.B \-w
to make this permanent and silent.
.TP
.B W05
The Windows helper stopped.
It is restarted automatically.
If this repeats, run the helper by hand (the message shows how) to see PowerShell's own error;
antivirus software or a PowerShell policy can block it.
.TP
.B W06
Windows has no connected adapter with an IPv4 address (reported by the helper).
Connect to the network.
.TP
.B W07
An upstream DNS server did not answer or cannot be reached.
Test it with
.IR "dig @ADDRESS example.com" .
.TP
.B W08
The last-known-good file or its directory cannot be written.
The daemon still works; it only loses the memory across restarts.
.TP
.B W10
A note about running as or without root.
.TP
.B W11
The log file cannot be opened.
.TP
.B W12
The pid file cannot be written.
.TP
.B W15
No mDNS reply came for a name, so the Windows resolver was tried (reported by the helper).
The other machine may be off, asleep, on another network, or blocked by a firewall on UDP port 5353.
Check from Windows with
.IR "ping NAME" .
.TP
.B W16
The worker process died and the supervisor restarted it.
Queries in flight at that moment were lost.
Run the daemon in the foreground, without
.BR \-D ,
to see why, and report the messages that precede this one.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start in the background, from the WSL boot command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wsl-dns-forward \-D \-P /run/wsl-dns-forward.pid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Test on an unprivileged port without touching resolv.conf:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wsl-dns-forward \-p 5353
dig @127.0.0.1 \-p 5353 myprinter.local
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a specific upstream and PowerShell location:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wsl-dns-forward \-D \-u 192.168.1.1 \-w /mnt/d/Windows/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
mDNS has no authentication: any machine on the local network can claim a
.I .local
name, so treat these addresses as only as trustworthy as that network.
The daemon accepts an address only from a reply whose owner name is the name that was asked,
and discards loopback, multicast and reserved addresses.
.PP
It listens on the loopback address only, answers nobody else, runs as an unprivileged user,
and passes the helper nothing but names made of letters, digits, hyphens and underscores.
Replies from the upstream server are accepted only from that server and only when the ID and question match.
.SH BUGS
Only A and AAAA records are answered for
.I .local
names.
DNSSEC data is not produced for those names, and reverse lookups (PTR) and service discovery (SRV, TXT) are not answered.
Windows must allow
.I powershell.exe
to run; an environment that blocks it (some managed or locked-down machines) cannot use this program.
.PP
Report problems at https://github.com/moebiusV/wsl-dns-forward/issues.
